1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,120
It's poured by the gallon, pumped and processed by a merry-go-round of high-tech, comes in

2
00:00:10,121 --> 00:00:17,080
different flavors, sits in the fridge, or on your shelf, and helps make the unlikeliest

3
00:00:17,081 --> 00:00:19,760
of non-food products.

4
00:00:19,761 --> 00:00:26,120
Found in the barnyard, under the waters of the ocean, or even in a field of beans.

5
00:00:26,121 --> 00:00:28,120
Put on your mustache.

6
00:00:28,121 --> 00:00:33,120
It's the untold story of milk on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:50,120
The United States produces over 21 billion gallons of milk every year.

8
00:00:50,121 --> 00:00:54,120
That's an average of 23 gallons for each American.

9
00:00:54,121 --> 00:01:00,300
But don't let this seemingly simple substance fool you.

10
00:01:00,301 --> 00:01:05,840
The milk we know best may come by the gallon or carton, but it's also a versatile wonder

11
00:01:05,841 --> 00:01:10,720
and plays a key role in hundreds of products.

12
00:01:10,721 --> 00:01:16,380
Virtually all of that milk comes from 60,000 dairy farms in the United States, courtesy

13
00:01:16,381 --> 00:01:23,360
of more than 9 million cows bred specifically for the purpose.

14
00:01:23,361 --> 00:01:28,940
Even for the past century, the aptly named milking machine has lent them a helping mechanized

15
00:01:28,941 --> 00:01:31,680
hand.

16
00:01:31,681 --> 00:01:34,860
The gadget grasping the cow's teat is called the cup.

17
00:01:34,861 --> 00:01:36,980
It works just like her hand.

18
00:01:36,981 --> 00:01:39,600
It has a pulsation in each one of these cups.

19
00:01:39,601 --> 00:01:42,820
It's just a squeezing and a pulling effect.

20
00:01:42,821 --> 00:01:47,060
And what that does, it applies pressure to the cow's udder, and that causes her to release

21
00:01:47,061 --> 00:01:49,800
her milk into this milk machine.

22
00:01:49,801 --> 00:01:55,260
And then once the milk gets into this bowl right here, it has a vacuum.

23
00:01:55,261 --> 00:02:02,120
And the vacuum will take it through the hose and into a jar.

24
00:02:02,121 --> 00:02:07,300
One farmer can hand milk six average cows per hour.

25
00:02:07,301 --> 00:02:14,140
Using milking machines, he can milk more than 100 cows at the same time.

26
00:02:14,141 --> 00:02:18,840
As this tried and true tech keeps the milk flowing, how the farmer treats the cow can

27
00:02:18,841 --> 00:02:21,940
affect the milk she gives.

28
00:02:21,941 --> 00:02:23,980
The better we treat the animals, the longer they're

29
00:02:23,981 --> 00:02:27,340
going to last and the more milk they're going to produce.

30
00:02:27,341 --> 00:02:31,380
And it's just what's best for the animal in return

31
00:02:31,381 --> 00:02:34,460
is best for us.

32
00:02:34,461 --> 00:02:38,380
7,500 contented cows populate the Larry Shahady Dairy Farm

33
00:02:38,381 --> 00:02:42,180
near Fresno, California.

34
00:02:42,181 --> 00:02:45,240
It's one of many farms taking milking to a new level

35
00:02:45,241 --> 00:02:50,240
that improves cow comfort and increases production.

36
00:02:50,241 --> 00:02:55,260
As many as 80 cows at a time can ride one of two bovine carousels

37
00:02:55,261 --> 00:02:57,180
called Day-Level Rototech parlors.

38
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:02,880
The ritual occurs twice every day.

39
00:03:02,881 --> 00:03:05,220
Dairy cows are creatures of habit.

40
00:03:05,221 --> 00:03:07,900
They like to do the same thing every day

41
00:03:07,901 --> 00:03:10,600
without any changes to their routine.

42
00:03:10,601 --> 00:03:12,480
They're very happy going to the milk barn

43
00:03:12,481 --> 00:03:14,060
at the same time each day.

44
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:17,160
The parlor works far more efficiently

45
00:03:17,161 --> 00:03:19,480
than an array of standard milking machines,

46
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:23,920
unleashing the flood of milk as much as 30% faster.

47
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:29,060
Workers lining the parlor near the entry point

48
00:03:29,061 --> 00:03:30,980
prep the cows after they hop aboard.

49
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:34,940
As the cows rotate to the workers' positions,

50
00:03:34,941 --> 00:03:38,980
they sanitize each cow's teeth with an iodine solution,

51
00:03:38,981 --> 00:03:40,380
then attach the cups.

52
00:03:40,381 --> 00:03:48,580
Once the actual unit is attached, the process is a vacuum line that comes in and stimulates

53
00:03:48,581 --> 00:03:52,720
the cow with pulsation in order to extract the milk from the teats.

54
00:03:52,721 --> 00:03:57,000
During that time, she'll basically be left alone for a period of time.

55
00:03:57,940 --> 00:04:00,900
Within about seven minutes, the milking is complete.

56
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:05,860
A flow sensor detects when Bessie is running on empty.

57
00:04:05,861 --> 00:04:15,400
When that flow sensor is triggered to be a lower flow, then it'll automatically take the units off, and then she'll be ready to leave.

58
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:22,680
Rotary parlors are ideal for large farms, efficiently milking a lot of cattle in a short time.

59
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:34,760
For cost-conscious farms with less than 500 cows, the Robotic Voluntary Milking System, or VMS, might just be the ticket.

60
00:04:34,761 --> 00:04:38,960
It helps eliminate the need for farmhands to lend the cows an assist.

61
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:46,200
With a voluntary milking system, it removes all those individuals from the parlor

62
00:04:46,201 --> 00:04:48,780
because we're bringing the milking machine out to the cows.

63
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:53,200
The voluntary system is something that the cows are truly at free choice

64
00:04:53,201 --> 00:04:54,840
to come and get milked when they want to.

65
00:04:56,100 --> 00:05:00,140
The BMS plays into a dairy cow's instinctive desire to deliver her milk.

66
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:04,380
If she withholds her milk for as little as two days,

67
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:06,680
She could dry up within one week.

68
00:05:08,180 --> 00:05:12,620
When a cow enters a BMS unit, it is scanned for height, weight,

69
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:17,680
and then a robotic arm will come in and image process the actual individual cow.

70
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:20,440
The entire process is automated.

71
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:23,220
Look, Bob, no human hands.

72
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:26,560
As high-tech as modern dairy equipment has become,

73
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:32,600
the creature it revolves around may be the greatest marvel of all.

74
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:39,800
today's dairy cow spends up to eight hours every day eating nearly 100 pounds of feed

75
00:05:40,920 --> 00:05:46,680
and drinking 80 gallons of water in order to produce up to 10 gallons of milk

76
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:54,600
seven days a week that's three times more productive than her forerunners just 50 years ago

77
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,520
one reason for this quantum leap in productivity is modern breeding

78
00:06:00,521 --> 00:06:04,160
Another is a more nutritious diet.

79
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:08,700
There's much more information available today.

80
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:13,900
We have a nutritionist that comes out to the dairy farm once a month and analyzes the feed

81
00:06:13,901 --> 00:06:20,160
and puts together a very efficient ration, combining proteins, carbohydrates, and fats.

82
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:27,160
A dairy cow starts producing milk at age two, after she delivers her first calf.

83
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:30,160
Farmhands usually separate mother and calf.

84
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:34,120
permanently within one hour of the birth.

85
00:06:34,121 --> 00:06:36,140
A key reason for this is to prevent the calf

86
00:06:36,141 --> 00:06:41,520
from introducing harmful bacteria to its mother's utter.

87
00:06:41,521 --> 00:06:46,200
Workers then bottle feed the calf with the mother's milk.

88
00:06:46,201 --> 00:06:49,180
After one month, the mother begins her professional life

89
00:06:49,181 --> 00:06:51,820
as a milk producer.

90
00:06:51,821 --> 00:06:55,980
The calf, it will transition to solid feed.

91
00:06:55,981 --> 00:07:02,200
A male might be sold as a beef cow, occasionally for veal, or go on to be a stud for breeding.

92
00:07:04,480 --> 00:07:10,360
A female will likely follow in her mother's footsteps, providing milk to the farmer.

93
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:18,040
They are going to be milked at least twice a day, seven days a week, 305 days straight.

94
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:20,120
They are off for 60 days.

95
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:24,540
They have their next baby calf, and they are going to start the milking process all over again,

96
00:07:24,541 --> 00:07:26,620
twice a day, 305 days straight.

97
00:07:27,260 --> 00:07:31,760
The milk she produces is a nutrient-rich liquid that's 87% water.

98
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:39,560
The process begins when four chambers in her stomach digest food and break down its nutrients.

99
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:42,580
The nutrients then enter her bloodstream.

100
00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:48,880
Within one hour, more than 400 gallons of blood has passed through her circulatory system

101
00:07:48,881 --> 00:07:54,140
and transported nutrients that help to become one gallon of milk in her mammary glands.

102
00:07:54,540 --> 00:08:00,940
Her mammograms are going to take the nutrients from this food and the water that she does

103
00:08:00,941 --> 00:08:06,020
eat and drink and turns it into the milk, basically for her baby calf, but because she

104
00:08:06,021 --> 00:08:08,580
gives so much, that's where we get the milk.

105
00:08:08,581 --> 00:08:15,680
A cow's milk exits her udder at approximately 90 degrees Fahrenheit.

106
00:08:15,681 --> 00:08:21,220
To safely preserve it until it can be transported to a processing plant, dairy farmers pump it

107
00:08:21,221 --> 00:08:26,780
to refrigerated storage tanks, like this one at Billings Farm in Woodstock, Vermont.

108
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:31,840
This is a pretty standard bulk tank that you would normally find on a small dairy farm.

109
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:34,120
It holds 600 gallons of milk.

110
00:08:34,440 --> 00:08:39,700
The bulk tank's job is to cool the milk down to about 37 or 38 degrees Fahrenheit.

111
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:46,040
The milk is picked up every 48 hours and sent off to a processing center to be made into dairy products.

112
00:08:47,880 --> 00:08:50,520
Although the cow is our most abundant source of milk,

113
00:08:51,220 --> 00:08:55,840
producing an average of 80 pounds per day with about 3 to 5 percent milk fat.

114
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,280
Milk flows from all of nature's mammals.

115
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:05,700
A human female produces roughly 2 pounds of milk a day.

116
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:09,760
The mouse, 5 hundredths of a pound.

117
00:09:11,660 --> 00:09:13,960
The pig, 13 pounds.

118
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:17,980
The horse, 30 pounds.

119
00:09:17,981 --> 00:09:24,980
But no mammal, not even the cow, can come close to the output of the humpback whale.

120
00:09:24,981 --> 00:09:32,480
A mother provides a whopping 1,000 pounds of milk to its calf every day, with 42% milk

121
00:09:32,481 --> 00:09:35,040
fat.

122
00:09:35,041 --> 00:09:39,840
Its milk also contains about twice the protein of most mammals, and nearly half the water

123
00:09:39,841 --> 00:09:41,720
content.

124
00:09:41,721 --> 00:09:46,080
The consistency, that of toothpaste, makes it easier for the humpback calf to consume

125
00:09:46,081 --> 00:09:50,280
in water.

126
00:09:50,281 --> 00:09:54,720
This treasure of nutrition helps the calf gain an off-the-chart 200 pounds every day

127
00:09:54,721 --> 00:09:59,200
for the first several weeks of its life.

128
00:09:59,201 --> 00:10:02,960
Holy cow!

129
00:10:02,961 --> 00:10:08,840
Among our more familiar bovine milk producers, one breed dominates.

130
00:10:08,841 --> 00:10:11,680
91% of all dairy cows are Holstein.

131
00:10:14,680 --> 00:10:17,880
The Shahidi farm stocks no other kind.

132
00:10:17,881 --> 00:10:19,640
Just one of these Holsteins can churn out

133
00:10:19,641 --> 00:10:24,120
23,000 gallons in a single year.

134
00:10:24,121 --> 00:10:25,980
They produce the highest amount of milk,

135
00:10:25,981 --> 00:10:27,780
and they produce the milk that is closest

136
00:10:27,781 --> 00:10:31,080
in nutrient composition that that would be found in fluid

137
00:10:31,081 --> 00:10:35,220
milk consumed in a bottle from a store.

138
00:10:35,221 --> 00:10:38,840
The Holstein may be the queen of today's barnyard.

139
00:10:38,841 --> 00:10:42,146
But 4,000 years ago, other cows ruled.

140
00:10:42,147 --> 00:10:52,240
Humans had already domesticated several breeds, including the predecessor to this impressive cow, the Angkol Watusi.

141
00:10:53,780 --> 00:11:00,800
Native to Africa, this breed continues to supply milk there today and has been dubbed the cattle of kings.

142
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:04,300
The Watusi are one of the oldest breeds on record.

143
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:08,440
They are in the pictorial graphs of the Egyptian pyramids.

144
00:11:09,640 --> 00:11:12,940
They were originally kind of an Egyptian longhorn,

145
00:11:12,941 --> 00:11:17,180
and about 2,000 years ago they were crossbred a little bit with the zebu,

146
00:11:17,340 --> 00:11:21,540
the longhorn zebu, and kind of made the Watusi breed.

147
00:11:23,820 --> 00:11:26,480
The Watusi flourished throughout arid Africa,

148
00:11:26,840 --> 00:11:28,520
partly because of its prominent horns.

149
00:11:28,521 --> 00:11:33,520
The reason for the large diameter horns is that helps cool their body.

150
00:11:33,521 --> 00:11:38,520
And afterwards, like 120 degrees, their blood flows through their horns like a radiator

151
00:11:38,521 --> 00:11:42,520
in a car and helps cool them.

152
00:11:42,521 --> 00:11:47,520
Herders considered their cows sacred and seldom slaughtered them for meat,

153
00:11:47,521 --> 00:11:53,520
figuring they could derive nearly as many nutrients from their milk.

154
00:11:53,521 --> 00:11:57,520
Many ancient African and Middle Eastern migrants spread into Europe,

155
00:11:57,521 --> 00:12:01,920
bringing cow's milk to new cultures.

156
00:12:01,921 --> 00:12:07,660
As populations moved across the Eastern European continent to Western Europe,

157
00:12:07,661 --> 00:12:10,760
more and more milk drinking happened.

158
00:12:10,761 --> 00:12:17,580
Centuries later, Europe's milk drinkers brought their cattle to the New World.

159
00:12:17,581 --> 00:12:27,080
In fact, it's those European breeds that power and industry now worth $27 billion.

160
00:12:27,081 --> 00:12:36,520
But how is the raw milk from thousands of farms transformed into over 19 million gallons of mainstream product every day?

161
00:12:38,440 --> 00:12:50,060
In 1997, a Holstein cow named Miranda Oscar Lucinda produced a record-setting 67,914 pounds of milk in one year.

162
00:12:51,020 --> 00:12:56,740
That's enough milk to accompany the cookies of over 124,000 kindergarteners.

163
00:12:57,080 --> 00:13:01,080
Milk will return on Modern Marvels.

164
00:13:01,081 --> 00:13:06,080
We now return to Milk on Modern Marvels.

165
00:13:06,081 --> 00:13:14,080
The United States dairy industry sells more than 7 billion gallons of fluid milk each

166
00:13:14,081 --> 00:13:16,080
year.

167
00:13:16,081 --> 00:13:22,080
To fill this massive demand, processing plants like Driftwood Dairy in Almonte, California,

168
00:13:22,081 --> 00:13:27,580
California must work around the clock seven days a week.

169
00:13:27,581 --> 00:13:32,580
We're always receiving milk because the cows are always producing milk.

170
00:13:32,581 --> 00:13:35,080
So our tankers are coming in constantly.

171
00:13:35,081 --> 00:13:44,080
Every day tankers deliver about 150,000 gallons of raw milk from nearby farms.

172
00:13:44,081 --> 00:13:49,080
Much of the milk that we have here was milked just four or five, six hours ago.

173
00:13:49,081 --> 00:13:51,080
So it's very fresh.

174
00:13:51,081 --> 00:13:56,680
Even so, Driftwood's lab analysts must test all the milk before the dairy accepts it.

175
00:13:59,700 --> 00:14:02,900
Fat and salt content has to fall within the plant standards.

176
00:14:03,940 --> 00:14:09,380
And the milk must be free of any antibiotics, since some people are severely allergic to them.

177
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:15,300
Antibiotics would only be present if given to milk-producing cows.

178
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:20,700
If it's positive, we will reject the load. It'll go back to the dairy farm.

179
00:14:21,080 --> 00:14:22,560
and we will not receive it.

180
00:14:23,480 --> 00:14:25,380
If Driftwood approves the load of milk,

181
00:14:26,220 --> 00:14:27,760
workers pipe it into the facility.

182
00:14:28,860 --> 00:14:31,220
The first task at hand is to separate it.

183
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:36,800
Raw milk naturally separates into its cream content and skim milk.

184
00:14:38,220 --> 00:14:40,820
The cream is the milk's concentrated fat source,

185
00:14:41,640 --> 00:14:43,540
while the skim is the nonfat liquid.

186
00:14:45,100 --> 00:14:48,660
The fat globules are less dense and more buoyant than the liquid,

187
00:14:49,320 --> 00:14:50,920
allowing the cream to float to the top.

188
00:14:51,080 --> 00:15:00,000
The spinning action of this machine, the centrifugal separator, simply accelerates the process,

189
00:15:00,001 --> 00:15:04,800
separating 6,000 gallons of milk every hour.

190
00:15:04,801 --> 00:15:09,380
The separator consists of layers of cone-shaped disks.

191
00:15:09,381 --> 00:15:14,560
They spin together around 7,000 revolutions per minute.

192
00:15:14,561 --> 00:15:19,160
This forces the heavier skim to gravitate to the outside while the cream flows down

193
00:15:19,161 --> 00:15:33,161
the center. The skim portion serves as the basis for the dairy's fluid milk.

194
00:15:33,265 --> 00:15:36,420
The cream will be

195
00:15:36,421 --> 00:15:42,240
blended back in with other ingredients inside a batch tank to achieve the desired taste and fat

196
00:15:42,241 --> 00:15:46,240
content in a variety of products, including flavored notes.

197
00:15:46,241 --> 00:15:51,368
Today we're making a drip-less strawberry splash.

198
00:15:51,369 --> 00:15:55,240
One of our ingredients was strawberry

199
00:15:55,241 --> 00:15:57,465
base. We add vitamins.

200
00:15:57,466 --> 00:16:04,240
They also add condensed skim for more protein, as well as cream for

201
00:16:04,241 --> 00:16:06,763
the right amount of butter fat.

202
00:16:06,764 --> 00:16:11,240
We add just enough sugar to make it good and sweet, but

203
00:16:11,241 --> 00:16:13,200
to harm our children.

204
00:16:13,201 --> 00:16:16,380
Though blended, the milk is still raw.

205
00:16:16,381 --> 00:16:20,260
It must be heated or pasteurized to kill virtually

206
00:16:20,261 --> 00:16:23,860
all the bacteria it contains.

207
00:16:23,861 --> 00:16:26,460
The milk, chilled to 38 degrees Fahrenheit,

208
00:16:26,461 --> 00:16:33,080
flows into a heat exchanger, which heats it to 162 degrees.

209
00:16:33,081 --> 00:16:35,880
The hot milk then moves into a holding tube for 15 and 1

210
00:16:35,881 --> 00:16:41,560
half seconds to meet the legal standard for pasteurization.

211
00:16:41,561 --> 00:16:50,440
We then pump it back through our pipes to our cooling section where we cool the milk

212
00:16:50,441 --> 00:16:53,880
back to 38 degrees.

213
00:16:53,881 --> 00:17:02,200
The pasteurization process takes less than 40 seconds and kills 99% of the milk's bacteria.

214
00:17:02,201 --> 00:17:08,440
Before the advent of pasteurization, most milk the consumers ingested was raw.

215
00:17:08,441 --> 00:17:13,380
Bacteria in the milk multiplied and caused spoilage, exposing millions to potentially

216
00:17:13,381 --> 00:17:15,960
deadly microorganisms.

217
00:17:15,961 --> 00:17:25,020
In 1862, French scientist Louis Pasteur pioneered a solution as he was attempting to solve the

218
00:17:25,021 --> 00:17:27,200
same spoilage problem for wine.

219
00:17:27,201 --> 00:17:33,900
He would preserve the quality of the beverage, yet inactivate the spoilage organisms.

220
00:17:33,901 --> 00:17:39,980
And that was really the basis of pasteurization, which then caught on in the dairy sector.

221
00:17:39,981 --> 00:17:45,580
By the 1930s and 40s, they started to recognize that in addition to preventing spoilage, it

222
00:17:45,581 --> 00:17:51,700
was also reducing the incidence of foodborne outbreaks associated with consumption of milk

223
00:17:51,701 --> 00:17:52,700
products.

224
00:17:52,701 --> 00:17:57,700
Today, over 95% of the milk we buy in the store is pasteurized.

225
00:17:57,701 --> 00:18:04,700
But most dairies, like driftwood, follow up pasteurization with a process called homogenization.

226
00:18:04,701 --> 00:18:12,700
In tanks like this one, milk fat breaks down into small, uniform particles throughout the milk.

227
00:18:12,701 --> 00:18:15,700
The way it works is real simple.

228
00:18:15,701 --> 00:18:20,700
We take this particle and slam it against the wall.

229
00:18:20,701 --> 00:18:28,700
wall and so we've got to set particles so very small that it can't rise to the top of the glass

230
00:18:29,260 --> 00:18:38,540
ever again after driftwood packages the milk it's stored in a refrigeration area at 32 degrees

231
00:18:38,541 --> 00:18:45,260
fahrenheit then trucks ship the milk to schools restaurants and other businesses throughout

232
00:18:45,261 --> 00:18:51,940
Southern California. Samples of the milk you enjoy at home have often been taste

233
00:18:51,941 --> 00:18:58,260
