295
00:23:16,400 --> 00:23:21,500
Over 1.5 billion pounds of butter are produced in the United States every year.

296
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:27,660
At facilities like this one in Issaquah, Washington.

297
00:23:28,440 --> 00:23:30,740
Behind me is the Dairy Gold Issaquah Creamery.

298
00:23:31,280 --> 00:23:33,380
This plant was built in 1909.

299
00:23:33,920 --> 00:23:39,880
Today it is producing over 10% of the butter manufactured in the United States.

300
00:23:41,100 --> 00:23:42,720
But what's in that butter?

301
00:23:43,560 --> 00:23:49,340
It's essentially a combination of a little water, a dash of salt, some milk protein,

302
00:23:49,840 --> 00:23:52,560
and a gut-busting 80% milk fat.

303
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:59,760
The fat and protein arrive at the Dairy Gold Creamery in the form of 1.5 million pounds

304
00:23:59,761 --> 00:24:06,180
of cream, arriving daily from four plants throughout the Northwest and Idaho.

305
00:24:06,181 --> 00:24:12,700
Like milk at a dairy processing plant, the cream undergoes pasteurization.

306
00:24:12,701 --> 00:24:18,620
Then it chills in a cooling tank for 12 hours at 50 degrees Fahrenheit, converting the liquid

307
00:24:18,621 --> 00:24:21,440
milk fat into a more solid consistency.

308
00:24:21,441 --> 00:24:26,440
The firmed up cream then makes its way to the churn.

309
00:24:26,441 --> 00:24:29,440
Our butter churn is one of the largest churns in the world.

310
00:24:29,441 --> 00:24:33,440
It can process over 50,000 pounds of cream in an hour.

311
00:24:33,441 --> 00:24:36,440
When the cream comes in at the top of the churn,

312
00:24:36,441 --> 00:24:40,440
it enters the beater section or primary churning chamber.

313
00:24:40,441 --> 00:24:45,440
Here, a large drum rotates at up to 1,500 RPM.

314
00:24:45,441 --> 00:24:50,440
This causes fat granules to form and begin to stick together.

315
00:24:50,441 --> 00:24:55,640
Next, the granules and the remaining liquid, buttermilk, drop into an adjacent chamber.

316
00:24:57,400 --> 00:25:01,420
Within the chamber, two large augers revolve in opposite directions.

317
00:25:02,440 --> 00:25:06,040
These augers compress the granules and force out the buttermilk.

318
00:25:07,500 --> 00:25:12,180
The resulting solid, now butter, squeezes through a plate with holes,

319
00:25:12,960 --> 00:25:16,260
separating it into strands that expose more of the butter's surface area.

320
00:25:17,260 --> 00:25:19,820
At the same time, it enters a vacuum chamber.

321
00:25:20,440 --> 00:25:27,640
The chamber removes 99% of the butter's oxygen, making it denser and less prone to spoilage.

322
00:25:28,200 --> 00:25:36,040
In a final chamber, salt is blended in, and the completed product oozes into a vat called the butterboat.

323
00:25:38,280 --> 00:25:43,620
The butterboat, then, is the main storage vessel where it's pumped out to our butter lines,

324
00:25:44,040 --> 00:25:49,160
either the large bulk packer or into quarters, patties, chips.

325
00:25:50,440 --> 00:25:52,040
or 68 pound boxes.

326
00:25:55,240 --> 00:25:57,840
The basic principles applied in this industrial scale

327
00:25:57,841 --> 00:26:03,680
operation date back centuries to an era when the common man

328
00:26:03,681 --> 00:26:04,940
made his own butter at home.

329
00:26:07,740 --> 00:26:09,880
These early butter makers started the process

330
00:26:09,881 --> 00:26:15,200
by allowing their milk to sit and sour for 24 hours

331
00:26:15,201 --> 00:26:18,400
until the cream floated to the surface.

332
00:26:18,401 --> 00:26:20,020
Then they carefully skimmed it off.

333
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,280
Churning the cream required some more basic tech

334
00:26:24,281 --> 00:26:25,960
and a strong hand.

335
00:26:27,040 --> 00:26:29,380
What I'm using is called a Daisy Churn,

336
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:31,440
which was patented in 1905.

337
00:26:32,060 --> 00:26:35,020
And what we do here is a series of simple machines

338
00:26:35,021 --> 00:26:37,220
with gears and a crank and a paddle,

339
00:26:37,620 --> 00:26:40,240
and it churns or stirs the cream up.

340
00:26:41,500 --> 00:26:43,420
Wondering why simply stirring the cream

341
00:26:43,421 --> 00:26:44,460
makes it so much thicker?

342
00:26:45,380 --> 00:26:47,440
It breaks the slippery outer shells

343
00:26:47,441 --> 00:26:51,840
of the cream's fat globules, exposing the sticky portion inside.

344
00:26:53,460 --> 00:26:57,240
All of a sudden, the fat particles are going to stick together.

345
00:26:58,100 --> 00:27:00,680
You just have to be patient and crank for a while.

346
00:27:01,680 --> 00:27:05,700
It all depends on the temperature of the cream.

347
00:27:06,440 --> 00:27:11,300
Sometimes it takes 15 minutes, and sometimes it takes 40 minutes.

348
00:27:13,080 --> 00:27:15,500
Housewives and children churned enough for the family,

349
00:27:15,501 --> 00:27:17,460
and a little extra to sell in town.

350
00:27:19,120 --> 00:27:21,080
At the turn of the 20th century,

351
00:27:21,960 --> 00:27:24,960
small creameries like the Billings Farm in Woodstock, Vermont

352
00:27:24,961 --> 00:27:28,640
were using innovative ways to produce butter in bulk.

353
00:27:30,300 --> 00:27:33,160
Today, the farm serves as an agricultural museum

354
00:27:33,161 --> 00:27:36,820
that offers programs demonstrating early butter-making methods.

355
00:27:38,440 --> 00:27:41,560
Step 1. Separate more cream faster.

356
00:27:42,760 --> 00:27:45,220
By cooling cans of milk in cold water,

357
00:27:45,500 --> 00:27:52,300
Workers allowed cream fat to firm up and float to the top in 11 hours instead of an entire day.

358
00:27:54,940 --> 00:27:59,760
We've just drawn off the skim milk, and that will be fed to the hogs.

359
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,720
As a byproduct of the butter-making process, skim milk was considered to be unfit for human consumption.

360
00:28:07,540 --> 00:28:12,280
While the skim ran off to a barrel, the cream made its way to a tempering bag.

361
00:28:15,500 --> 00:28:18,460
And finally to what was called a swing churn,

362
00:28:18,461 --> 00:28:20,260
driven by a water-powered motor.

363
00:28:23,460 --> 00:28:25,540
It required no paddle.

364
00:28:25,541 --> 00:28:27,400
Instead, the churn agitated the cream

365
00:28:27,401 --> 00:28:28,940
by swinging it back and forth.

366
00:28:33,360 --> 00:28:37,200
After a few hours, the farm had fresh butter to pack up.

367
00:28:37,201 --> 00:28:41,800
And we will firmly pack it in to be sure

368
00:28:41,801 --> 00:28:45,280
that we have that pound of butter that that consumer

369
00:28:45,281 --> 00:28:46,831
so interested in.

